  <h1>What to Do After a Dog Bite</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Get medical treatment immediately</h2><p>Dog bites cause deep puncture wounds prone to infection. Seek emergency care even for seemingly minor bites. Medical records establish the injury and are the foundation of your claim. Follow all treatment recommendations &mdash; gaps in care give insurers ammunition to minimize damages.</p>
  <h2>2. Identify the dog and owner</h2><p>Get the owner&rsquo;s name, address, and phone number. Confirm the dog&rsquo;s vaccination status &mdash; if unknown or unvaccinated, you will need the rabies post-exposure prophylaxis protocol (four injections over 14 days). Document the dog&rsquo;s breed, size, and any identifying features.</p>
  <h2>3. Report the bite</h2><p>Report the incident to local animal control or the health department. This creates an official record, triggers investigation of the owner&rsquo;s insurance and vaccination status, and documents the incident before memory fades or evidence is lost.</p>
  <h2>4. Photograph injuries</h2><p>Take photographs of your wounds immediately and throughout healing. Scarring photographs taken over weeks or months document the full extent of permanent disfigurement. Photograph the location where the bite occurred, any restraints (or lack thereof), and the dog if possible.</p>
  <h2>5. Consult a personal injury attorney</h2><p>Homeowners insurers are experienced at minimizing claims. An attorney familiar with dog bite cases can identify the correct liability theory, deal with the insurer, and build the damages case (including future plastic surgery costs). Most work on contingency &mdash; no fee unless you win.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-dog-bite-claims-work">how claims work</a> and <a href="/guides/dog-bite-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
